The Fabry–Perot interferometer (FPI) is a widely employed structure in optical fiber sensing, particularly suitable for measuring temperature, vibration, pressure, and refractive index. FPI-based sensors are advantageous for single-point detection and are well suited for harsh environments due to their compactness and robustness. A typical FPI sensor comprises two parallel reflective surfaces, one at the fiber–air interface and the other at a sensing diaphragm, forming a low-finesse optical cavity. Variations in external stimuli, such as acoustic waves or mechanical pressure, cause deformations in the diaphragm, which in turn modulate the optical path length and alter the interference pattern of the reflected light. Multiple reflections occur between the two surfaces, creating interference fringes sensitive to cavity length changes. The optical phase Φ of the reflected wave is given by [ 60 ]: 4 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
