Here, S denotes the refractive index sensitivity, d m  is the effective thickness of the surrounding medium, and L d  is the decay length of the evanescent field [ 80 ]. In optical fiber-based SPR systems, surface plasmon excitation is enabled by total internal reflection at the fiber core–cladding interface. Guided light within the core undergoes reflection at incident angles between the critical angle θc and 90°, generating evanescent waves in the cladding. A plasmonic metal layer, typically 50 nm of gold or silver, is deposited on a stripped section of the fiber cladding. These evanescent waves couple with the surface plasmon modes at the metal–analyte interface, giving rise to resonance absorption, marked by a dip in the transmission spectrum. Compared to prism or grating couplers, optical fibers provide a compact and efficient platform for SPR excitation. However, due to angular dispersion in guided modes, the resulting resonance spectrum is broader, which affects sensitivity and detection limits. To fabricate a fiber-optic SPR probe, a section of the cladding is removed and coated with a plasmonic material. When a polychromatic beam is launched into the fiber, SPR is excited in the coated region where the analyte is present, and spectral shifts are monitored to detect refractive index variations. The performance of SPR fiber sensors is closely tied to the plasmonic material used. Silver offers the highest field enhancement due to its low dielectric loss in the visible range, though it suffers from poor chemical stability [ 81 ]. Gold, on the other hand, provides chemical robustness and is thus widely used in practical SPR sensors, despite its slightly lower plasmonic performance. SPR-based fiber sensors have been applied to diverse analytes, including small molecules and biological species. However, a key limitation is the shallow penetration depth (~ 200 nm) of the evanescent field, which reduces sensitivity to large biomolecules such as proteins [ 82 ]. For 